  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(-\\frac{1}{3}).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-3{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =9{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-27{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2034}(-\\frac{1}{3}),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2034}(-\\frac{1}{3})=-27\\cdot {e}^{-3\\cdot (-1/3)}=-27e.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(-\\frac{1}{3})=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-27e)=-\\frac{9e}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}$ is $-\\frac{9e}{2}.$",
